# BEKW-CODE // RESILIENCE FORGE
## QUAKEFORGE (Seismic) + TIDEFORGE (Tsunami) — Disaster-Resistant Variants of the FORGE Chassis

**Status:** Full design spec, grounded in established structural/coastal engineering practice
**Canon tie-ins:** FORGE SYSTEM (base chassis — Channel A/B/C/D interlocking framing), THE WALL PLATFORM (breakaway panel logic), HAVEN (existing storm refuge, different failure mode — wind/surge, not seismic/tsunami)
**Design thesis:** Earthquakes and tsunamis fail buildings in opposite ways. A quake needs the structure to move without breaking. A tsunami needs the *water* to move through the structure without the structure resisting it. Most disaster-proofing fails because it tries to make a building rigid and impermeable — exactly wrong for both problems. QUAKEFORGE and TIDEFORGE both work by giving the hazard somewhere to go instead of fighting it head-on.

---

## PART 1 — QUAKEFORGE (Seismic)

### 1.1 Base Isolation, Not Rigid Foundation
The single highest-leverage seismic intervention is decoupling the structure from ground motion rather than trying to out-build it. QUAKEFORGE mounts the FORGE chassis on a base isolation layer between foundation and first-floor Channel A/B grid:
- **Friction pendulum bearings** (curved sliding surface, structure re-centers under gravity after motion) at every primary column point — well-proven technology, not experimental
- Isolation layer sized to shift the building's natural period well clear of typical destructive earthquake frequencies (site-specific, requires local seismic data — this is the one number in the whole system that can't be standardized)
- A flexible utility loop (Channel A power/data, Channel D plumbing) crosses the isolation gap in slack service loops, not rigid runs — same "everything is a repairable cassette" philosophy as the rest of FORGE, just applied to the gap itself

### 1.2 Ductile Frame, Not Stiff Frame
FORGE's existing interlocking chassis is already modular at the joint level, which is the right starting point — the fix is making the joints *yield in a controlled, predictable place* rather than the members themselves:
- **Sacrificial yield fuses** at designated Channel B mechanical-lock points: an intentionally weaker connector element that deforms first and absorbs energy, replaceable after an event without touching the load-bearing members around it — same swap-the-part-not-the-building logic as FORGE's existing skin-panel repair philosophy
- Cross-bracing follows a chevron/eccentric pattern (allows the brace itself to buckle predictably rather than transferring full force into the frame)
- Every yield fuse gets the same piezo sensor layer FLOORFORGE already uses for impact — post-quake, the building can report exactly which fuses need replacing instead of requiring a full structural inspection

### 1.3 Mass and Damping
- TMD (tuned mass damper) siting at roof level for taller QUAKEFORGE builds, sized to counter-oscillate against the structure's dominant sway mode
- Where a THERMWEAVE or WATTWORKS mass is already present (mechanical room, water tank, elevated garden bed), the design deliberately uses it as dual-purpose damping mass rather than adding dead weight solely for damping

### 1.4 Non-Structural Survival
Most seismic deaths and injuries come from non-structural failure — falling ceiling material, unsecured heavy objects, blocked egress — not full collapse:
- Wall Platform panels (GREENWALL, POWERWALL, etc.) lock into the chassis with the same mechanical interlock used everywhere else in FORGE, which already resists panel ejection under lateral shake far better than typical drywall-on-stud construction
- Egress paths run inside AMBIENTFORGE's denser interior sensing zone specifically so post-quake path-clearing sensors (debris/blockage detection) are already wired in, not retrofitted

