## PART 2 — TIDEFORGE (Tsunami)

### 2.1 The Core Principle: Let the Water Through
A rigid wall facing a tsunami either fails catastrophically or transfers the full hydrodynamic load into the foundation. Established tsunami-resistant design (the same logic behind FEMA P-55/ASCE 7 Chapter 6 guidance) instead **elevates the occupied structure and lets the wave pass underneath and through the ground level**, rather than fighting it.

- **Open pile/breakaway ground floor** — TIDEFORGE's ground level is either open piloti (structure elevated on the FORGE chassis's own column grid, no enclosing walls) or, where ground-floor use is required, enclosed with **breakaway Wall Platform panels** engineered to release from their Channel B lock under a calibrated hydrodynamic load threshold well below what would damage the primary structure — the wall becomes the sacrificial element, same swap-not-rebuild logic as everywhere else in FORGE
- **Occupied floors elevated above calculated maximum runup + freeboard margin** for the specific coastline (site-specific number, same caveat as seismic period — no universal height, has to be modeled against local bathymetry and historical runup data)
- **Foundation/pile system resists uplift and scour**, not lateral wave force — piles driven deep enough that the ground floor's own breakaway design means they're never asked to resist the full wave load in the first place

### 2.2 Vertical Evacuation
Where horizontal evacuation to high ground isn't realistic in the available warning time, TIDEFORGE structures are rated as **vertical evacuation refuges** in their own right — same category as a standalone tsunami vertical evacuation structure, but built into the standard occupied building instead of requiring a separate dedicated structure:
- Refuge floor elevation independently verified above maximum credible runup, not just "upper floor is probably fine"
- Refuge floor structurally isolated from ground-floor breakaway zone by a full Channel A/B/C/D chassis transition, so a ground-floor failure event can't propagate damage upward
- External stair access redundant to interior stairs, sited so it isn't the first thing the wave hits

### 2.3 Buoyancy and Anchorage
A structure that survives the wave load but then floats off its foundation is still a failure:
- Below the occupied refuge floor, any enclosed volume is either flood-vented (breakaway panels again) or explicitly designed for controlled buoyant uplift resistance — anchored, not sealed-and-hoping
- Anchorage sized against full flotation force of any sealed volume in the structure, not just against lateral wave drag

### 2.4 What This Doesn't Claim
Site-specific numbers — actual isolation period, actual runup elevation, actual freeboard margin — depend entirely on local seismic and bathymetric data and can't be generalized in a framework doc. This spec establishes the chassis-level design pattern (isolate/yield-controlled for quake, elevate/let-through for tsunami); the numbers that make a specific building on a specific coastline actually rated come from a site engineer, same as they would for any code-compliant structure anywhere.

---

## 3. Where This Plugs Into Existing Canon
Both QUAKEFORGE and TIDEFORGE are chassis-level variants, not new buildings — a Forgehouse, a Wall Platform home, or any FORGE structure sited somewhere seismically or tidally active adopts these as configuration choices on the same underlying system, the same way SUNFORGE vs. AMBIENTFORGE is a configuration choice rather than a different product line.
